00:07:25.857 --> 00:07:26.690
YURI: Yeah, awesome.

00:07:26.690 --> 00:07:30.175
Yeah, all right, let's carry on.

00:07:30.175 --> 00:07:34.580
KARTHI: Cool, so as I mentioned,
before we get into the demo,

00:07:34.580 --> 00:07:38.672
let's just look at
this very simple flow.

00:07:38.672 --> 00:07:40.130
Essentially, what
we're going to do

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:42.830
is we're going to
create a toy example.

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:46.100
And it starts with this
simple driver program

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:49.310
that we created that dumps
a whole bunch of orders

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:50.632
into Pub/Sub.

00:07:50.632 --> 00:07:53.090
These are orders that have some
basic information, as we'll

00:07:53.090 --> 00:07:56.370
see, like the name of the
person that made the order,

00:07:56.370 --> 00:07:59.000
as well as a widget ID
that indicates that they

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:00.520
ordered this particular widget.

00:08:00.520 --> 00:08:02.220
So it'll go through Pub/Sub.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.880
It'll be processed by Dataflow.

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:07.670
And then from Dataflow,
we'll put it into BigQuery.

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:09.440
And so that's the store phase.

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:13.760
And once it lands in BigQuery,
this is where a business--

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:16.640
let's say an e-tailer
or a retailer--

00:08:16.640 --> 00:08:19.310
can start to make sense
of the kinds of purchasing

00:08:19.310 --> 00:08:22.880
decisions that are
being made by customers.

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:26.750
And one of, obviously,
the key insights

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:30.170
there is, how are customers
deciding what to buy, right?

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:32.720
This is a question
that often gets asked.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:35.419
So we'll look at
some ways of creating

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:39.500
models that help us predict some
of those purchasing decisions.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:45.080
And we'll use something called
Datalab, which is essentially

00:08:45.080 --> 00:08:48.230
a hosted Jupyter Notebook,
and this is usually

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:49.790
the realm of data scientists.

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:54.260
And from Datalab, what we'll do
is once we've honed the model,

00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:59.180
we'll deploy that model to
a fully-managed serverless

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:03.652
machine learning system called
Cloud Machine Learning Engine.

00:09:03.652 --> 00:09:05.819
Does that sound good, guys?

00:09:05.819 --> 00:09:06.860
NICK: Sounds great, yeah.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:08.234
Definitely looking
forward to it.

00:09:08.234 --> 00:09:09.470
Let's dig into the data.

