00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:00.380
And this is where--

00:12:00.380 --> 00:12:03.300
a data scientist is
perfectly comfortable

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:05.600
dealing with a data
warehouse like BigQuery.

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:07.890
However, they also
want the power

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:10.830
of something like
Jupyter Notebook.

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:13.410
A lot of data scientists work
in R, in Python, et cetera.

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:19.440
And in this case, we'll focus
on Python with Jupyter Notebook.

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:24.460
So Jupyter Notebook has
its ancestry in IPython.

00:12:24.460 --> 00:12:26.560
They renamed it.

00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:31.980
And now you have all of
that power available to you

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:34.890
as a hosted service.

00:12:34.890 --> 00:12:40.130
And the name of that
service is Cloud Datalab.

00:12:40.130 --> 00:12:43.410
And the way you interact with
Datalab is actually with a CLI.

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:48.930
There isn't a UI within the GCP
console that you can get to.

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:51.030
So essentially what
you would have to do

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:54.600
is install the Datalab CLI.

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:57.930
And once you get to the
CLI, you have the ability

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:03.160
to create a Datalab instance,
and then you can connect to it.

00:13:03.160 --> 00:13:08.110
So as I mentioned, you
have Create, Connect, List,

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:08.610
et cetera.

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:13.685
So let's look at Datalab List to
see how many Datalab instances

00:13:13.685 --> 00:13:14.310
I have running.

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:20.120
As you can see here, I
have one instance running,

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:21.650
and it's in US central.

00:13:21.650 --> 00:13:24.200
And by the way, you can also
specify the machine type

00:13:24.200 --> 00:13:26.067
that you would like.

00:13:26.067 --> 00:13:27.650
For certain kinds
of number crunching,

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:30.530
you may want a bigger instance.

00:13:30.530 --> 00:13:34.250
In this case, I've gone
with a standard-4, which

00:13:34.250 --> 00:13:37.700
is a little bit bigger than
what you'd get by default.

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:39.260
So let me pause
there real quick.

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:41.000
Nick and Yuri, do you
guys have anything

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:43.220
that you want me to
focus on before I get

00:13:43.220 --> 00:13:46.420
to the actual Notebook itself?

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:47.920
NICK: Actually, I
have one question.

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:50.110
So you're talking about,
from the overall pipeline

00:13:50.110 --> 00:13:52.750
perspective, the data's going
to be streaming through Pub/Sub

00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:54.760
and then going through Dataflow.

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:56.485
And then at the
end, it's basically

00:13:56.485 --> 00:13:59.410
pipelined into the data
warehouse named BigQuery.

00:13:59.410 --> 00:14:03.880
So it supports streaming
directly into the--

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:07.660
basically into your BigQuery
table using a streaming API.

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:09.520
Do you mind talking
about the pros and cons

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:13.240
of going your route of streaming
through Pub/Sub and then

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:16.510
Dataflow versus
directly to BigQuery?

00:14:16.510 --> 00:14:18.910
KARTHI: Yeah, that's
a great point.

00:14:18.910 --> 00:14:22.720
So one reason that
comes to mind as

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:24.370
to why you should
go with Pub/Sub

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:26.620
is if you want more
flexibility, let's say,

00:14:26.620 --> 00:14:30.220
in filtering or
transforming the data--

00:14:30.220 --> 00:14:38.330
if you have requirements that
require heavy transformations

00:14:38.330 --> 00:14:40.959
against the data before it
lands in a store like BigQuery,

00:14:40.959 --> 00:14:42.500
I would definitely
recommend that you

00:14:42.500 --> 00:14:47.140
go with putting
Dataflow in the middle.

00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:49.970
For simple ingest
scenarios, I think

00:14:49.970 --> 00:14:51.920
it's perfectly OK to
leave out Dataflow.

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:55.806
But usually in an enterprise
scenario that's not the case.

00:14:55.806 --> 00:14:57.680
NICK: Yeah, that makes
sense, especially when

00:14:57.680 --> 00:14:59.150
you're scaling up.

00:14:59.150 --> 00:14:59.650
OK.

00:14:59.650 --> 00:15:00.483
How about you, Yuri?

00:15:00.483 --> 00:15:02.897
Any questions that you have
before we get kicked off?

00:15:02.897 --> 00:15:03.480
YURI: No, sir.

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:05.420
I want to see how this
all is going to work.

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:06.169
NICK: There we go.

00:15:06.169 --> 00:15:06.785
Cool.

00:15:06.785 --> 00:15:08.350
KARTHI: Awesome.

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:12.660
OK, so let's get to
the Notebook itself.

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:16.780
So real quick, I already have a
Notebook running and connected.

00:15:16.780 --> 00:15:19.960
So when you run Datalab
connect and give it

00:15:19.960 --> 00:15:23.470
the name of the actual
Datalab instance, what it does

00:15:23.470 --> 00:15:27.670
is it creates an SSH tunnel, and
then it automatically pops up

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:29.320
a browser window--

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:30.270
like I've done here.

00:15:30.270 --> 00:15:33.060
And you can see here that the
address is localhost 8081.

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:36.830
And you can change this port to
another port if you so choose,

00:15:36.830 --> 00:15:40.210
but this makes it really easy
to connect to this essentially

00:15:40.210 --> 00:15:42.320
separate VM with all
the goodies installed.

00:15:42.320 --> 00:15:44.390
So we're talking NumPy.

00:15:44.390 --> 00:15:47.320
We're talking
TensorFlow-- everything

00:15:47.320 --> 00:15:50.710
that a data
scientist would want.

00:15:50.710 --> 00:15:58.320
And to continue with a quick
tour of what this Notebook

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:04.620
gives you, if you were to start
this up, what you can do is--

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:06.300
let me just-- sorry.

00:16:06.300 --> 00:16:09.600
Let me just scroll
up here and show you

00:16:09.600 --> 00:16:11.730
that what you have
here with Datalab

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:17.970
is essentially a managed service
that anybody who has access

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:19.680
to that project can connect to.

00:16:19.680 --> 00:16:22.710
So every time somebody
opens a Notebook,

00:16:22.710 --> 00:16:26.340
what they get is a separate
kernel that runs the Notebook

00:16:26.340 --> 00:16:27.520
that they're looking at.

00:16:27.520 --> 00:16:29.910
And once you're
done, essentially,

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:35.760
if you're seeing
that the resource

00:16:35.760 --> 00:16:37.650
usage for that
instance is heavy,

00:16:37.650 --> 00:16:39.660
you can shut down
that Notebook so

00:16:39.660 --> 00:16:44.620
that you're freeing up
memory, CPU usage, et cetera.

00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:46.530
YURI: So Karthi, let
me ask you a question.

00:16:46.530 --> 00:16:48.450
I imagine that some
folks on the line

00:16:48.450 --> 00:16:51.050
may or may not be familiar.

00:16:51.050 --> 00:16:54.071
They probably-- I'm sure they've
heard of Python, but Jupyter--

00:16:54.071 --> 00:16:55.820
it's a bit of a different
paradigm, right?

00:16:55.820 --> 00:16:59.840
Lots of people are familiar
writing Python code in an IDE.

00:16:59.840 --> 00:17:02.900
Tell us a little bit about
why do people use Jupyter.

00:17:02.900 --> 00:17:06.849
What has it given that
command line Python doesn't?

00:17:06.849 --> 00:17:08.140
KARTHI: Another great question.

00:17:08.140 --> 00:17:11.040
So one of the big things
is you essentially get

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:18.130
this ability to mix
markdown and actual code.

00:17:18.130 --> 00:17:20.040
So that even if
you, yourself, were

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:23.190
the only one working
in this Notebook,

00:17:23.190 --> 00:17:26.140
you have self-documenting code.

00:17:26.140 --> 00:17:29.220
So if I were to open
up this guy here--

00:17:29.220 --> 00:17:31.900
Introduction to Notebooks.

00:17:31.900 --> 00:17:34.790
And by the way, this is
all built into Datalab.

00:17:34.790 --> 00:17:36.790
You can open up these
examples and play with it.

00:17:36.790 --> 00:17:38.700
So you can see, this
looks like a web page.

00:17:38.700 --> 00:17:40.170
Very well-documented.

00:17:40.170 --> 00:17:43.800
You find a cell and you
put in your Python code.

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:46.770
And you hit Control-Enter,
or you can just click on Run,

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:48.690
and it actually runs the code.

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:49.920
And all of it is documented.

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:50.600
You can save it.

00:17:50.600 --> 00:17:52.600
You can share these
notebooks with other people.

00:17:52.600 --> 00:17:55.201
So it's the collaboration and
the self-documenting aspect

00:17:55.201 --> 00:17:57.450
of it, I would say, is one
of the most powerful things

00:17:57.450 --> 00:18:01.230
about doing it this way
versus running it in an IDE

00:18:01.230 --> 00:18:02.320
like Python.

00:18:02.320 --> 00:18:03.361
YURI: Awesome, thank you.

00:18:03.361 --> 00:18:03.708
KARTHI: Does that help?

00:18:03.708 --> 00:18:04.940
YURI: Yeah, very much so.

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:09.450
All right, so now, how do we use
data in Pub/Sub or in BigQuery

00:18:09.450 --> 00:18:11.830
within Jupyter?

00:18:11.830 --> 00:18:13.410
KARTHI: Yeah, that's
definitely one

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:19.410
of the significant
capabilities with Datalab

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:21.540
is the integration is built in.

00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:23.765
So for example,
there are these--

00:18:23.765 --> 00:18:25.890
Jupyter Notebook, by the
way, gives you the ability

00:18:25.890 --> 00:18:27.510
to run these magic commands.

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:29.280
That's what they're called.

00:18:29.280 --> 00:18:31.560
And what the Datalab
folks have done

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:33.810
is they've enhanced
that to add the ability

00:18:33.810 --> 00:18:36.990
to integrate with GCP
services like BigQuery.

00:18:36.990 --> 00:18:40.290
So this is a magic command here.

00:18:40.290 --> 00:18:41.910
It's %%bq query.

00:18:41.910 --> 00:18:45.540
And then what I can
do is submit a query,

00:18:45.540 --> 00:18:47.760
like I would into
the BigQuery UI,

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:49.844
and then I get the
results right here.

00:18:49.844 --> 00:18:51.510
And that integration
is really powerful.

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:55.020
Because what you can then
do on top of this is now

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:56.445
you can bring in
the power of all

00:18:56.445 --> 00:19:00.930
of the other number crunching,
visualization capabilities.

00:19:00.930 --> 00:19:04.560
So as an example,
what I've done here

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:07.660
is I've created this variable.

00:19:07.660 --> 00:19:09.510
Sorry, it's called
DF in this case.

00:19:09.510 --> 00:19:11.570
DF to signify that
it's a data frame.

00:19:11.570 --> 00:19:14.800
So the data scientist
amongst the audience

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:18.240
will recognize data frames.

00:19:18.240 --> 00:19:24.360
And data frames, obviously,
come with really powerful data

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:27.390
science capabilities, for
lack of a better phrase.

00:19:27.390 --> 00:19:29.430
So you can populate
this data frame.

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:31.680
And then you can go
on, as I've done here,

00:19:31.680 --> 00:19:33.390
to do some more visualization.

00:19:33.390 --> 00:19:36.540
So what I've done is I've
taken this data frame,

00:19:36.540 --> 00:19:39.000
and essentially what
I'm trying to do

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:43.130
here is model the relationship
between the person who put it

00:19:43.130 --> 00:19:46.020
in order and the
type of widget it

00:19:46.020 --> 00:19:48.780
is just to see if I
can actually use it

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:50.590
for predictive analytics.

00:19:50.590 --> 00:19:55.500
So this both goes to illustrate
the power of combining all

00:19:55.500 --> 00:19:58.140
the GCP services-- as
you've mentioned, Yuri--

00:19:58.140 --> 00:20:02.290
along with what Datalab adds
in terms of capabilities.

00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:06.060
So in this case, I take the
data out of the BigQuery query

00:20:06.060 --> 00:20:07.710
that I did, and then
I'm plotting it.

00:20:07.710 --> 00:20:13.530
So you can see that there's
a linear relationship

00:20:13.530 --> 00:20:16.050
between the name of
the person that put

00:20:16.050 --> 00:20:17.480
in an order and the widget ID.

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:19.355
And obviously, this is
a contrived example,

00:20:19.355 --> 00:20:20.730
but this gives
you an idea of how

00:20:20.730 --> 00:20:22.620
you can do some
visualization directly

00:20:22.620 --> 00:20:24.620
against the data of BigQuery.

00:20:24.620 --> 00:20:25.520
YURI: Very cool.

00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:26.978
All right, so I
think that gives us

00:20:26.978 --> 00:20:31.210
a great perspective on the data
scientist's experience in GCP,

00:20:31.210 --> 00:20:34.890
using data from BigQuery
as it first arrives--

00:20:34.890 --> 00:20:36.475
either directly from
Pub/Sub streaming

00:20:36.475 --> 00:20:39.750
or from the Dataflow transform.

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:41.910
What other persona
do you have in mind

00:20:41.910 --> 00:20:45.840
for taking advantage of
data from different steps

00:20:45.840 --> 00:20:48.730
of the pipeline
over the lifecycle?

00:20:48.730 --> 00:20:51.180
KARTHI: Yeah, so
if we were to go

00:20:51.180 --> 00:20:56.340
beyond just this
ability to experiment,

00:20:56.340 --> 00:21:03.780
you have the analyst who has to,
let's say, actually implement--

00:21:03.780 --> 00:21:06.090
actually, before I
get to the analyst,

00:21:06.090 --> 00:21:07.830
let's talk about the
system architect who

00:21:07.830 --> 00:21:15.700
has to, maybe, implement
the predictive analytics

00:21:15.700 --> 00:21:20.030
that a data scientist has
gleaned from this graph here.

00:21:20.030 --> 00:21:23.190
So the data
scientist says, oh, I

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:25.030
have a light bulb moment here.

00:21:25.030 --> 00:21:28.870
I know that this is how
customers are making decisions

00:21:28.870 --> 00:21:29.830
on what to order.

00:21:29.830 --> 00:21:32.860
So let us now actually
create a machine

00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:40.750
learning model that will
take all this training data--

00:21:40.750 --> 00:21:46.970
all this gold, basically-- and
suggest widgets for customers

00:21:46.970 --> 00:21:47.470
to buy.

00:21:47.470 --> 00:21:53.230
So to implement this at scale,
what a system architect would

00:21:53.230 --> 00:21:59.080
have to do is essentially go
to this training data that's

00:21:59.080 --> 00:22:00.670
in BigQuery and
say, OK, I'm going

00:22:00.670 --> 00:22:04.030
to do some feature engineering.

00:22:04.030 --> 00:22:07.870
And to do this at
scale, a product

00:22:07.870 --> 00:22:11.890
that we recently released
is called Dataprep.

00:22:11.890 --> 00:22:15.890
So if you're cool with it,
Yuri, shall we move to Dataprep

00:22:15.890 --> 00:22:18.712
and talk about what
that can do for us?

00:22:18.712 --> 00:22:19.710
YURI: Yeah, absolutely.

00:22:19.710 --> 00:22:21.720
I'm really interested
in seeing how--

00:22:21.720 --> 00:22:25.070
now that we understand the
life of a data scientist

00:22:25.070 --> 00:22:30.330
on GCP, I think we want to
see, how does an architect

00:22:30.330 --> 00:22:31.744
benefit from this data as well?

00:22:31.744 --> 00:22:33.910
NICK: Yeah, the biggest
challenge that I've run into

00:22:33.910 --> 00:22:37.580
frequently is just that the
data comes in all jumbled,

00:22:37.580 --> 00:22:41.800
and there's really no
way of simply cleaning it

00:22:41.800 --> 00:22:43.480
up and preparing it.

00:22:43.480 --> 00:22:46.720
So we'll have to
see what you can do.

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:48.210
KARTHI: Awesome.

00:22:48.210 --> 00:22:49.810
Cool.

00:22:49.810 --> 00:22:53.090
So as we discussed, we have
this data that's in BigQuery.

00:22:53.090 --> 00:22:59.440
So let's pull up Dataprep
and extract, essentially,

00:22:59.440 --> 00:23:04.400
what are called features that'll
help us do some predictions.

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:06.820
And a picture is worth
a thousand words,

00:23:06.820 --> 00:23:09.340
so let's actually create
something called a flow.

00:23:09.340 --> 00:23:10.890
What a flow is--

00:23:10.890 --> 00:23:13.090
you can think of
it as a transform.

00:23:13.090 --> 00:23:17.714
It helps you take raw data
and shape it into something

00:23:17.714 --> 00:23:19.630
that you can use for a
machine learning model.

00:23:19.630 --> 00:23:21.730
Because oftentimes, you
can't just take raw data

00:23:21.730 --> 00:23:24.370
and apply training to it,
because the data's probably not

00:23:24.370 --> 00:23:27.500
going to be in a
shape that you want.

00:23:27.500 --> 00:23:35.050
So let's do a very uncreative
name for this flow here.

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:39.550
And what we're
going to do is we're

00:23:39.550 --> 00:23:41.110
going to add a data
set that is going

00:23:41.110 --> 00:23:43.870
to be the source data set
for this transformation

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:45.790
that we talked about.

00:23:45.790 --> 00:23:50.460
And for that, I'm
going to pick a source.

00:23:50.460 --> 00:23:52.500
So as you can see,
to start off with,

00:23:52.500 --> 00:23:55.410
you can either
pull a GCS object,

00:23:55.410 --> 00:23:58.050
or you can go to
BigQuery as the source,

00:23:58.050 --> 00:24:01.090
and you can pick a table.

00:24:01.090 --> 00:24:03.390
In this case, we're going
to pick this table, which

00:24:03.390 --> 00:24:07.320
is where the order data landed.

00:24:07.320 --> 00:24:11.610
Once we've picked the
source, what we can then do

00:24:11.610 --> 00:24:19.630
is we can edit the way the
transformation functions.

00:24:19.630 --> 00:24:23.040
And we can do it in a
visual, UI-based fashion,

00:24:23.040 --> 00:24:25.590
and this is one of the key
capabilities of Dataprep.

00:24:25.590 --> 00:24:27.090
You're not writing
a bunch of code--

00:24:27.090 --> 00:24:29.048
which, there's nothing
wrong with writing code.

00:24:29.048 --> 00:24:31.740
But a lot of times it's
hard for people to picture

00:24:31.740 --> 00:24:33.000
what the data looks like.

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:36.750
And that's one of the really
powerful aspects of Dataprep.

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:38.400
So we have the source data.

00:24:38.400 --> 00:24:41.160
So we create something
called a recipe.

00:24:41.160 --> 00:24:43.560
And a recipe is something
that, essentially,

00:24:43.560 --> 00:24:45.910
applies to the data.

00:24:45.910 --> 00:24:48.930
And let's edit this
recipe to tell it what

00:24:48.930 --> 00:24:51.665
to do with the raw data here.

00:24:51.665 --> 00:24:55.840
As you can tell, it's loading
the demo table that I created.

00:24:55.840 --> 00:24:57.840
And I'm sure you'll
recognize all these columns.

00:24:57.840 --> 00:24:59.465
It's the same columns
that I showed you

00:24:59.465 --> 00:25:00.570
in that Python class--

00:25:00.570 --> 00:25:03.327
Order ID, Order Created
Date, et cetera.

00:25:03.327 --> 00:25:05.035
And right off the bat,
what you'll notice

00:25:05.035 --> 00:25:08.730
is Dataprep does a
good job of adding

00:25:08.730 --> 00:25:11.230
visual indicators to the data.

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:16.850
So for example, it'll tell
me this name, Jennifer Smith,

00:25:16.850 --> 00:25:19.329
occurs 0.04% in
the whole data set.

00:25:19.329 --> 00:25:20.370
So these are really cool.

00:25:20.370 --> 00:25:22.570
But what's even more
powerful is it'll tell me,

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:27.900
hey, there are different shapes
for customer phone number,

00:25:27.900 --> 00:25:33.180
and also, there are 1,925 rows
that don't have customer phone.

00:25:33.180 --> 00:25:34.890
So you can see
there's a lot of power

00:25:34.890 --> 00:25:38.040
here in giving you the
ability to filter--

00:25:38.040 --> 00:25:41.422
leave out things that
may not be helpful.

00:25:41.422 --> 00:25:43.490
YURI: That's a really
important point, there,

00:25:43.490 --> 00:25:46.630
is that you can
quickly understand,

00:25:46.630 --> 00:25:48.280
what does the data look like?

00:25:48.280 --> 00:25:49.490
Do I have outliers?

00:25:49.490 --> 00:25:51.110
Are there gaps?

00:25:51.110 --> 00:25:55.340
So let's say that the thing
you were trying to figure out

00:25:55.340 --> 00:25:58.330
is the location
of your customers.

00:25:58.330 --> 00:26:00.910
Phone number might be a really
important piece of information

00:26:00.910 --> 00:26:04.690
there, because the area code
gives you very precise location

00:26:04.690 --> 00:26:08.034
information if you don't have
GPS data or anything like that.

00:26:08.034 --> 00:26:09.950
And if that's a key part
of what you're doing,

00:26:09.950 --> 00:26:11.991
then having some significant
portion of your data

00:26:11.991 --> 00:26:15.280
not have a phone number makes
it somewhat less useful.

00:26:15.280 --> 00:26:16.492
KARTHI: That's right.

00:26:16.492 --> 00:26:17.950
NICK: And just out
of curiosity, it

00:26:17.950 --> 00:26:20.350
seems like this does a lot
of the transformational piece

00:26:20.350 --> 00:26:22.810
that Dataflow also drives.

00:26:22.810 --> 00:26:27.520
So what's the relationship
between Dataprep and Dataflow?

00:26:27.520 --> 00:26:29.900
KARTHI: Great point.

00:26:29.900 --> 00:26:32.800
Dataprep is actually
built on top of Dataflow.

00:26:32.800 --> 00:26:35.980
And the reason that's
significant is that once you--

00:26:35.980 --> 00:26:39.810
as we'll do here-- once
you create this transform,

00:26:39.810 --> 00:26:43.570
it is actually run
within Dataflow,

00:26:43.570 --> 00:26:46.530
which means we can deal
with hundreds of terabytes--

00:26:46.530 --> 00:26:48.950
or even petabytes-- of
data because of the scalar

00:26:48.950 --> 00:26:52.100
capabilities that Dataflow
brings to the table.

00:26:52.100 --> 00:26:52.820
NICK: Awesome.

00:26:52.820 --> 00:26:55.110
That's interesting.

00:26:55.110 --> 00:26:57.240
KARTHI: Yeah, so let's
actually-- sorry, go ahead.

00:26:57.240 --> 00:27:00.060
YURI: I'm sorry
to interrupt you.

00:27:00.060 --> 00:27:02.430
In the interest of keeping
this to a manageable length--

00:27:02.430 --> 00:27:05.220
because I think we could easily
be here for an hour or two

00:27:05.220 --> 00:27:06.900
hours talking about everything--

00:27:06.900 --> 00:27:09.750
I think this visibility
with Dataprep

00:27:09.750 --> 00:27:11.940
gives folks really excellent
understanding of how

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:15.480
they can go about doing some
of that data wrangling and data

00:27:15.480 --> 00:27:16.020
clean up.

00:27:16.020 --> 00:27:17.660
And then, like
you said, creating

00:27:17.660 --> 00:27:22.350
those transforms that would
then make this data more useful

00:27:22.350 --> 00:27:26.426
or actually clean
it up automatically.

00:27:26.426 --> 00:27:27.800
So let me ask you,
to shift gears

00:27:27.800 --> 00:27:29.800
