00:27:46.660 --> 00:27:48.520
and then also the
system architect.

00:27:48.520 --> 00:27:50.770
In this case, this
is the person that's

00:27:50.770 --> 00:27:55.000
implementing the entire system
and then bringing it online

00:27:55.000 --> 00:27:57.650
so it can do online predictions
and things of that nature.

00:27:57.650 --> 00:28:04.960
So I'll segue to that persona
by talking about how Dataprep

00:28:04.960 --> 00:28:07.690
can be leveraged to generate
the raw data to do the machine

00:28:07.690 --> 00:28:08.300
learning.

00:28:08.300 --> 00:28:12.730
So in this case, I've
picked out all the raw data

00:28:12.730 --> 00:28:16.480
that doesn't have missing
order IDs for whatever reason.

00:28:16.480 --> 00:28:18.470
And then I run
this Dataflow job.

00:28:18.470 --> 00:28:20.800
And as Nick pointed out--

00:28:20.800 --> 00:28:22.930
sorry, this Dataprep job.

00:28:22.930 --> 00:28:27.370
As Nick pointed out,
just to show you,

00:28:27.370 --> 00:28:30.730
when you run a Dataprep job, it
actually shows up in Dataflow,

00:28:30.730 --> 00:28:32.080
as you can see here.

00:28:32.080 --> 00:28:35.950
And this just confirms the fact
that underneath the covers,

00:28:35.950 --> 00:28:38.590
Dataprep is actually
using Dataflow.

00:28:38.590 --> 00:28:41.680
OK, so with that said,
let's now jump back

00:28:41.680 --> 00:28:47.991
