00:28:48.490 --> 00:28:51.340
how they would take a
machine learning model

00:28:51.340 --> 00:28:53.560
that the data scientist
has put together,

00:28:53.560 --> 00:28:59.050
and how they will generate
a prediction engine

00:28:59.050 --> 00:29:02.090
for predictive analytics.

00:29:02.090 --> 00:29:03.940
YURI: All right, sounds great.

00:29:03.940 --> 00:29:05.470
KARTHI: Awesome.

00:29:05.470 --> 00:29:06.990
Cool.

00:29:06.990 --> 00:29:08.740
So this is going to
be very light in terms

00:29:08.740 --> 00:29:11.187
of actual machine
learning modeling.

00:29:11.187 --> 00:29:13.270
I mean, this is a very
unsophisticated model, just

00:29:13.270 --> 00:29:14.394
for demonstration purposes.

00:29:14.394 --> 00:29:16.867
So keep that in mind.

00:29:16.867 --> 00:29:18.700
Anyway, so the data
scientist has determined

00:29:18.700 --> 00:29:20.616
that there's a very
simple linear relationship

00:29:20.616 --> 00:29:23.410
between the name of the
person that's making the order

00:29:23.410 --> 00:29:26.980
and the widget that
ends up getting ordered.

00:29:26.980 --> 00:29:31.120
So what we're going
to do is we will

00:29:31.120 --> 00:29:34.720
take the cleansed
data that Dataprep

00:29:34.720 --> 00:29:37.190
puts into a GCS bucket.

00:29:37.190 --> 00:29:39.220
It's in the CSV file.

00:29:39.220 --> 00:29:41.240
Let me select it here.

00:29:41.240 --> 00:29:43.750
And with that,
what we will do is

00:29:43.750 --> 00:29:49.690
we will create a training data
set as well as a test data set.

00:29:49.690 --> 00:29:54.400
And this is actually
how TensorFlow typically

00:29:54.400 --> 00:29:56.380
works is you have to
have a training data set,

00:29:56.380 --> 00:30:00.700
and then you train your
model with a certain number

00:30:00.700 --> 00:30:03.520
of iterations, and then you test
that model with the test data

00:30:03.520 --> 00:30:07.600
set to make sure that
things aren't out of whack.

00:30:07.600 --> 00:30:08.100
Cool.

00:30:08.100 --> 00:30:12.180
So the way you do that--
without going into too many

00:30:12.180 --> 00:30:13.080
of the details--

00:30:13.080 --> 00:30:18.020
is you basically--
as I mentioned,

00:30:18.020 --> 00:30:19.880
you basically create
the training data set

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:22.010
and the test data set,
and then you leverage

00:30:22.010 --> 00:30:23.270
something called an estimator.

00:30:23.270 --> 00:30:28.850
This is built into
TensorFlow, and you

00:30:28.850 --> 00:30:31.700
have a variety of estimators
that you can pick from.

00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:40.100
In this case, we'll use the
simple linear regression model,

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:44.840
here, to train, and
we'll set up inputs

00:30:44.840 --> 00:30:48.520
that come from the training
data set that we talked about.

00:30:48.520 --> 00:30:51.300
And essentially, after
that, we say, hey,

00:30:51.300 --> 00:30:54.980
let's run this training for
300 steps and one evaluation

00:30:54.980 --> 00:30:58.610
that goes against the test
data set that I mentioned.

00:30:58.610 --> 00:31:01.640
With all of that
put into this task

00:31:01.640 --> 00:31:10.290
Python file, what we can then
do is try out the model locally.

00:31:10.290 --> 00:31:12.200
And all I have to
do is run the model.

00:31:12.200 --> 00:31:14.240
This is, by the way, not
running on ML Engine,

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:18.050
even though it is
referencing ML Engine.

00:31:18.050 --> 00:31:21.200
What this is doing is
taking the training data

00:31:21.200 --> 00:31:22.850
and running it in
my Datalab instance.

00:31:22.850 --> 00:31:26.210
So that's important to remember.

00:31:26.210 --> 00:31:31.420
Once that's satisfactory,
we're happy with the model

00:31:31.420 --> 00:31:34.480
performance, things like
that, we can actually

00:31:34.480 --> 00:31:36.894
run it in the cloud.

00:31:36.894 --> 00:31:38.310
And the way the
flow would go is--

00:31:38.310 --> 00:31:40.250
so the system
architect, let's say,

00:31:40.250 --> 00:31:43.180
would try this out in the cloud.

00:31:43.180 --> 00:31:46.340
And they would leverage
the power of the GCP Cloud.

00:31:46.340 --> 00:31:50.920
They have the ability to pick
a whole bunch of scale tiers.

00:31:50.920 --> 00:31:53.800
As some of you
may know, training

00:31:53.800 --> 00:31:56.230
is often the most
time-consuming aspect

00:31:56.230 --> 00:31:59.080
of doing predictive
analytics, because it requires

00:31:59.080 --> 00:32:01.360
some serious horsepower.

00:32:01.360 --> 00:32:05.480
And this is where being able
to tell Google Cloud Machine

00:32:05.480 --> 00:32:07.480
Learning Engine, hey, I
want to use 10 machines,

00:32:07.480 --> 00:32:09.670
or I want to use three GPUs--

00:32:09.670 --> 00:32:11.560
you can specify all
of that here when

00:32:11.560 --> 00:32:13.185
you're doing the
training in the cloud.

00:32:13.185 --> 00:32:16.680
You may bring in terabytes
of data to do that training.

00:32:19.210 --> 00:32:23.880
So then the training is done.

00:32:23.880 --> 00:32:25.860
You also, then, have
the ability to look

00:32:25.860 --> 00:32:27.990
at the characteristics of--

00:32:27.990 --> 00:32:28.750
I apologize.

00:32:28.750 --> 00:32:30.090
I have to rerun this again.

00:32:30.090 --> 00:32:34.080
But basically, if you guys
are familiar with TensorBoard,

00:32:34.080 --> 00:32:37.470
you have the ability to look
at where the model lands

00:32:37.470 --> 00:32:39.630
and all the characteristics
of the training,

00:32:39.630 --> 00:32:42.000
and you can inspect
it with TensorBoard.

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:45.240
So that'll tell you, hey,
this is the loss function.

00:32:45.240 --> 00:32:49.000
This is how long
it took to run--

00:32:49.000 --> 00:32:51.330
things of that nature.

00:32:51.330 --> 00:32:55.420
Once you have run the model
and you're happy with it,

00:32:55.420 --> 00:32:57.840
you can deploy it.

00:32:57.840 --> 00:32:59.490
And what does deployment mean?

00:32:59.490 --> 00:33:03.204
So deployment has to do with
actually having Cloud Machine

00:33:03.204 --> 00:33:03.870
Learning Engine.

00:33:03.870 --> 00:33:05.411
Once again, this is
fully serverless.

00:33:05.411 --> 00:33:09.790
We're not talking about
individual computers

00:33:09.790 --> 00:33:10.990
or your own managed cluster.

00:33:10.990 --> 00:33:12.864
We're talking about a
managed service hosting

00:33:12.864 --> 00:33:17.350
the model for you, and then
you can have it do predictions.

00:33:17.350 --> 00:33:21.260
And the way that works is
you essentially tell it, hey,

00:33:21.260 --> 00:33:26.620
this is where my model is
after training is complete.

00:33:26.620 --> 00:33:30.880
In this case, it
landed in this--

00:33:30.880 --> 00:33:33.610
when I say "it," machine
learning engine--

00:33:33.610 --> 00:33:35.980
after the training is
complete, landed the model

00:33:35.980 --> 00:33:39.070
in this GCS bucket
that you specified.

00:33:39.070 --> 00:33:42.190
And basically, you
can take that model

00:33:42.190 --> 00:33:44.980
and then use it
to do predictions.

00:33:44.980 --> 00:33:51.110
And what we're going to do
here is basically pass it

00:33:51.110 --> 00:33:53.459
